 "drivingQuestion": "What is ḥosen?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Abaye's gloss replaces Rav Yosef's.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.20b.7",
   "daf": "20b",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "וְלֹא בְּחוֹסֶן. אָמַר רַב יוֹסֵף: נְעוֹרֶת שֶׁל פִּשְׁתָּן. אֲמַר לֵיהּ אַבָּיֵי: וְהָכְתִיב ״וְהָיָה הֶחָסוֹן לִנְעוֹרֶת״, מִכְּלָל דְּחוֹסֶן לָאו נְעוֹרֶת הוּא! אֶלָּא אָמַר אַבָּיֵי: כִּיתָּנָא דִּדְיִיק וְלָא נְפִיץ.",
   "en": "The mishna taught further that «one may not» light «with ḥosen. Rav Yosef said:» Ḥosen is «tow,» thin chaff that falls off the stalk «of» combed «flax. Abaye said to him: Isn’t it written: “And the ḥason shall be as tow”» (Isaiah 1:31)? «By inference, ḥosen is not tow. Rather, Abaye said:» Ḥosen is «flax whose» stalk «was crushed» but «not» yet «combed.» The threads in the stalk are still covered by a shell and therefore do not burn well."
  }
 ],
